window.docContents[51].resolve({"/Huber-rings-and-Tate-rings/#Adic-Spaces-___-Lean-blueprint--Huber-rings-and-Tate-rings":{"contents":"A topological ring  is a Huber ring (or -adic ring) if it admits a\npair of definition: an open subring  together with a finitely\ngenerated ideal  such that the powers  form a neighbourhood\nbasis of  in . In Adic-Spaces the pair is the structure\n and the predicate is the class .\nFollowing Wedhorn Definition 6.1.\n\nA Tate ring is a Huber ring () containing a topologically\nnilpotent unit  with  — a\npseudo-uniformiser, playing the role of a uniformising parameter in\nnon-archimedean geometry. In Adic-Spaces this is the class .\nFollowing Wedhorn Definition 6.10.\n\nA pseudo-uniformiser of a topological ring  is a unit  that is\ntopologically nilpotent, . In Adic-Spaces: the predicate\n and the bundled type .\nA Tate ring is precisely a Huber ring possessing one ().\nFollowing Wedhorn Definition 6.10.\n\nFor a pair of definition  (), each ideal power\n is open in  () and the ring of definition \nis bounded in  (). These two facts say that  is an\nopen bounded subring whose -adic filtration realises the topology — the defining\ndata of a Huber ring. Following Wedhorn §6.\n\nIn a Tate ring  (, ) the set\n of topologically nilpotent elements is open.\n\nLet  be a pseudo-uniformiser. Since , the sets\n (images of a ring of definition) shrink to , so for large  the\nneighbourhood  of  consists of topologically nilpotent elements; as\n is a unit this neighbourhood translates to show  contains an\nopen set around each of its points. Hence  is open. Following Wedhorn §6.\n\n","context":"Adic Spaces — Lean blueprint","header":"1. Huber rings and Tate rings","id":"/Huber-rings-and-Tate-rings/#Adic-Spaces-___-Lean-blueprint--Huber-rings-and-Tate-rings"}});